+## Selector audit 43012272038 (campaign slot 01, LEARNING_PROCESS, task P-001)
+
+Scope: does explicit highest-value focused-task selection improve learning efficiency relative to the prior opportunistic queue choice?
+
+**Finding 1 — the selector is explicit and reconstructible, not a queue walk.** `campaign_controller.py:198-225` ranks the OPEN tasks for the agent's role by a lexicographic ordinal key over seven declared qualitative fields (`expected_information_gain`, `downstream_leverage`, `falsification_power`, `verifiability`, then `effort`, `complexity`, `execution_risk`, then `task_id`) and `:311` takes `candidates[0]`. Every selection is recorded with its comparison set in `campaign_status.selection_history` (`:373-381`). Recomputing the key by hand for the six OPEN LEARNING_PROCESS tasks reproduces the recorded order exactly: P-001, P-002, P-004, P-003, P-006. The role filter, not the benefit ranking, was decisive here: R-001..R-005 were OPEN with an equally HIGH benefit vector but belong to the other role, so agent 01 could not select them. The selector behaves as designed and is auditable.
+
+**Finding 2 — the comparative efficiency claim is not identifiable.** The controller selector has exactly one selection event (`campaign_status.selection_history` length 1; `global_agent_counter.json` shows a single campaign). The six "frontier-first demonstrated" activations (37263008074, 37304873966, 37314710995, 37318950814, 37394344201, 37414038601) were selected by the worker under the LEARNING_STATE strategy delta, not by `task_rank`; the separate lookback-sweep activation 37361000967 has no row of its own in the recent-history table and is recorded only inside the momentum row and the Decision line above. Verification practice also co-evolved with the rule rather than holding constant: independent recomputation already existed before it (`verify_aapl_stats.py` for the MA-crossover statistics; `verify_regime_adaptive.py` and `verify_dd_independent.py` for the 2026-10-05 MA work), and the standardized per-cell check+verifier pair only becomes the norm from 37263008074 onward. The prior opportunistic era also produced a decisive falsification with two independent paths (37257258857 / 37260572520). A superiority claim therefore cannot be separated from era and tooling confounds with the available durable evidence. Recorded as **no demonstrated advantage**, not as a refutation.
+
+**Finding 3 — correction of a durable over-claim.** The Decision line above previously read "RETAIN (verified)". The evidence verifies operational application (right cell selected, decisive verdict, independent recomputation), not the delta's expected effect (more information per unit of worker time). The label above is narrowed accordingly; the underlying figures are unchanged. The same over-claim survives in `state/activation_status.json` (activation 37414038601, `acceptance`); it is preserved there as that activation's own claim rather than rewritten.
+
+**Finding 4 — one counterexample to the selection rule as written.** Momentum was admitted at 37318950814 and then absorbed three further activations (37361000967 lookback sweep 3/5/10; 37394344201 lookbacks 20/60 plus a gap recharacterization; and the queued R-001 cost test). Each was literally not an equivalent re-test, so "no equivalent re-tests" holds; but each refined an already-admitted cell, which `LEARNING_EFFICIENCY.md` §1 asks the search to avoid ("rather than repeatedly refining the most interesting recent result"). The ordinal key has no field that expresses "this cell is already admitted", so the selector cannot detect this drift; the drift originates in the frontier's own `Next` field. Handed to P-002 as evidence; not fixed here, because no selector-level bottleneck is demonstrated by one observation.
+
+**Decision: RETAIN the selector unchanged** (deterministic, role-scoped, auditable, no observed bottleneck attributable to it) and record the comparative-efficiency question as UNVERIFIED rather than verified. A stronger conclusion requires a second selection event under the same gate stack.
+
+## Cost-sensitivity R-001 (campaign slot 02, HIGHER_ORDER_OBJECTIVE, run 43012272038)
+
+Scope: bounded cost-sensitivity check for lookback 3/5/10 short-horizon momentum under the repository's existing realistic cost model, with a matched (cost-bearing) coin-flip null. No signal rule, universe, window or cost figure was changed after any number was seen.
+
+**What ran.** `research/checks/momentum_cost_sensitivity.py` evaluated a predeclared cost grid `k x` the repository's own "realistic costs" configuration (`examples/ma_crossover_real_data.py:131-135`: commission_per_trade 2.0, commission_per_share 0.003, slippage_cents 2.0, slippage_proportional 0.0005) at k = 0.0 / 1.0 / 2.0, for lookbacks 3/5/10 over all 10 collected tickers, through the existing walk-forward regime-segmentation gate (`bt.stress_segments`, train 252 / test 84 / warmup 60 / overlap 60, 4 volatility blocks, min 400 bars/segment). Artifact: `state/check_artifacts/momentum_cost_sensitivity_results.json`. Verification: `research/checks/verify_momentum_cost_sensitivity.py` -> `state/check_artifacts/momentum_cost_sensitivity_verification.json`.
+
+**Verified.** Manifest 10/10 OK; preflight PASSED; leakage + fill-equity gate PASS on AMZN/JPM at realistic cost; determinism (AMZN/JPM, all lookbacks x all levels) r1 == r2. Grid outcome as produced: edge cells (REGIME_STABLE with all segment medians > 0) k=0.0 EDGE 17 / NO_EDGE 13; k=1.0 EDGE 18 / NO_EDGE 12; k=2.0 EDGE 18 / NO_EDGE 12; per-asset class counts COST_ROBUST 7, COST_FRAGILE 0, COST_ELIMINATED 3. Independent verifier: Gate 1 fresh `walk_forward` + `noise_benchmark` recomputation of all 90 cells MATCH; Gate 2 the k=0 column reproduces the existing `momentum_lookback_sweep_results.json` on all 30 cells MATCH; Gate 3 classification and per-level edge counts re-derived MATCH.
+
+**The grid result is not interpretable, and the reason is the finding.** Candidate segment medians were essentially unchanged across cost levels (e.g. MSFT lb5 [0.052, 0.072] at k=0/1/2; META lb5 [0.073, 0.067, 0.042] at all three levels; TSLA medians moved +0.001) while the cost-matched null moved materially (META lb5 null medians [-0.015, 0.045, -0.090] -> [-0.023, 0.044, -0.095]; MSFT lb5 [-0.013, -0.029] -> [-0.034, -0.030]). A candidate that pays no material cost while the null pays full turnover cost cannot be a directional trading rule. Gate 4 (added after gates 1-3 exposed this, and confined to diagnosing the test's premise) confirms why: `research/backtest/regime_stability.py::momentum_signals` computes `ret = mean(log(closes[i-lookback+1 : i+1]))` — the mean LOG PRICE over the window, not a return. For closes > 1 that quantity is strictly positive, so the emitted weight is +1 at every bar after the warmup window. Measured over the collected universe: 27/30 cells are single-sign (AAPL/MSFT/GOOGL/AMZN/META/TSLA/JPM/JNJ/XOM long-only, 1 sign change = the 0 -> +1 entry), and the 3 NVDA cells flip sign once only where the window mean log price crosses zero (range [-1.777, +5.443], i.e. "price above/below $1", not return momentum). Realized turnover of the tested series is 1.9x-6.4x per year, which is daily notional maintenance, not directional rebalancing.
+
+**Classification: COST_ROBUSTNESS_NOT_IDENTIFIED (signal premise defect).** The cost grid does not measure the cost robustness of a momentum rule, because the tested series is a constant long position (a US large-cap beta bet) on 9/10 assets.
+
+**Consequence for the admitted momentum evidence (qualification, not a re-adjudication).** The momentum rows above must be read as QUALIFIED: what was admitted as "short-horizon momentum" is a constant long exposure to each asset over 2009-2026, and the falsified reversal class is the same construction with the opposite constant sign. Two durable observations corroborate this reading and were not produced by this activation: (1) the k=0 medians in the existing lookback-sweep artifact are IDENTICAL across lookbacks 3/5/10 for every asset (e.g. AAPL [0.071, 0.054, 0.092, 0.069] at all three lookbacks), which the prior activation read as lookback-robustness but is the signature of a lookback-independent signal; (2) the previously reported -0.99 correlation between reversal and momentum daily P&L is what two constant opposite positions must produce. This activation did not re-run, re-score or overwrite any prior cell, verdict or figure; the prior rows are preserved verbatim and only the interpretation of what was tested is corrected here. The 20-day breakout cell is unaffected (its threshold rule uses `max(closes)`), and the cross-sectional classes are unaffected (they use true `close[i]/close[i-lookback]` returns).
+
+**What is not claimed.** No claim that short-horizon return autocorrelation is absent on this universe: that hypothesis was never tested here, because the signal used does not implement it. No claim that costs are irrelevant: only that a ~3x/year-turnover constant position is insensitive to the declared cost model up to 2x.
+
+**Decision: RETAIN** the gate stack (predeclared grid + check/verifier pair + independent recomputation) — it is what detected an implausible result and forced the premise audit. The cost-sensitivity question itself is closed as NOT_IDENTIFIED pending a corrected signal.
+
+**Next:** repair `momentum_signals` to the sign of the previous N-day log return, with a unit test that fails on a single-sign series, then re-run the momentum cell and the R-001 cost grid on the corrected signal. Until that repair exists, no cost-robustness claim about short-horizon momentum is admissible.
